WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.260
TLS I/O is not identical to plain socket I/O.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:15.160
A TLS library may need to read or write encrypted records, update internal state, process alerts, complete handshake work, or report retry conditions.

3
00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:22.380
Application code should follow the library's documented contract instead of assuming that one call maps directly to one complete application message.

4
00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.500
Partial progress is normal.

5
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:29.080
A TLS read may return less application data than the parser wants.

6
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.100
A TLS write may accept only part of the application data.

7
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.440
Nonblocking integrations may report that the operation should be retried when the socket is readable or writable.

8
00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:45.140
The application needs state that can resume without duplicating, losing, or reordering data.

9
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.980
Buffer ownership should be explicit.

10
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.720
Encrypted buffers belong to the TLS layer or transport integration.

11
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.020
Plaintext buffers belong to the application protocol layer.

12
00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:06.440
Sensitive application data should be kept only as long as needed, bounded by maximum sizes, and cleared where appropriate under the team's coding and platform guidance.

13
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.960
Nonblocking I/O and event loops require careful coordination.

14
00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:17.140
The event loop should know whether the TLS layer is waiting for read readiness, write readiness, or application action.

15
00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:21.480
Handshake, data transfer, and shutdown can all have retry behavior.

16
00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:27.100
Mixing TLS state with parser state without a clear contract makes failures hard to review.

17
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.340
TLS shutdown has protocol meaning.

18
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:35.320
A clean shutdown can differ from a transport close or an error.

19
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:43.420
The application should understand how the library reports close notifications, unexpected closure, partial transfer, and cleanup requirements.

20
00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:47.920
This helps avoid treating incomplete communication as a successful exchange.

21
00:01:48.200 --> 00:02:01.160
Cleanup should be predictable on every path: successful close, handshake failure, validation failure, timeout, parser rejection, transport error, and application shutdown.

22
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:09.260
Free TLS objects, close sockets or handles, release buffers, and record useful diagnostics from normal code paths.

23
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:15.520
Lifecycle discipline keeps protected communication from becoming a source of leaks or ambiguous state.
